<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE suite SYSTEM "https://testng.org/testng-1.0.dtd">

<!--
  Trimio ACCOUNT-ENUMERATION suite — the endpoints that must answer identically whether or not
  an address has an account.

  Run, AGAINST A FRESHLY STARTED BACKEND:
    cd ~/StudioProjects/trimio/backend && (restart node server.js)
    mvn test -DsuiteXmlFile=src/test/resources/suites/security-enumeration-testng.xml \
             -Dfirebase.webApiKey=… -Ddb.password=… -DretryCount=0

  WHY THIS IS NOT IN security-testng.xml ANY MORE.

  It was, filed in the block commented "LAST: the tests that deliberately trip the limiter" —
  next to its own opposite. These tests do not trip the limiter, they are starved by it, and the
  arithmetic makes that unavoidable inside the main suite.

  middleware/authRateLimit.js builds ONE credentialLimiter instance, keyed per IP, 10 requests
  per 15 minutes, and routes/authRoutes.js and routes/passwordRoutes.js BOTH mount it. So every
  /auth/login in the suite spends budget that /password/forgotPassword needs. Measured on a full
  run: 9 /auth/login calls land before this block starts, checkUserExists adds 2 more (the
  limiter sits in front of firebaseAuth, so even its 401s count), and forgotPassword arrives as
  request 12 of a 10-request allowance. Both of its calls returned 429, the test cannot compare
  a known address against an unknown one when every answer is "Too many attempts", and it
  skipped — in every run this week.

  Reordering does not fix it. Put this first and the four calls it makes plus the suite's nine
  logins is still 13 against a cap of 10, so the 429s simply move onto SEC-01 through SEC-07
  instead. One skip becomes several.

  A separate suite against a fresh process is the fix, because the limiter is in memory: a
  restart zeroes it, and these four calls then sit comfortably inside the shipped allowance.
  NOTE WHAT IS *NOT* DONE HERE — the caps are not raised and no limiter is bypassed. The suite
  runs against the shipped 10-per-15-minutes exactly as production has it. Verified: with a
  fresh limiter all three pass, and forgotPassword answers 200 for a registered address and 200
  for an unknown one, which is the indistinguishability being asserted.

  The brute-force and lockout tests stay in security-testng.xml. They SHOULD exhaust the budget;
  that is their subject.
-->
<suite name="Trimio Account-Enumeration Suite" parallel="false" verbose="1">

    <listeners>
        <listener class-name="org.example.listeners.TestListener"/>
    </listeners>

    <test name="SEC-05 — account enumeration">
        <classes>
            <class name="org.example.tests.security.AccountEnumerationTest"/>
        </classes>
    </test>

</suite>
